[[0,1322599,0,1,0,1,"2016-10-03T10:06:34","vonclausewitz","",[],"<p><a href=\"http://metro.co.uk/2016/09/29/young-women-at-staggeringly-high-risk-of-anxiety-and-depression-6161497/\">Here's an article</a> (<a href=\"http://archive.is/DaZ3s\">archive</a>) about a study which found that 1-in-4 young women, aged 16 to 24, are depressed. For men in the same age group, it's only 1-in-10. It's a UK study, but I suspect this is a trend throughout the West.</p>\n<p>I've seen articles and studies before about women ending up unhappy later in life, as a result of choices they made while young. But I don't think I've ever seen anything them being unhappy while still young. And the explanations put forth for the older women (mostly, that they end up alone) don't seem to work for the younger women. So what's going on?</p>\n<p>Also, why am I posting this? MGTOW is <strong>not</strong> about what women do or how they feel, so why is this relevant? </p>\n<p>I'm posting this to suggest to you that getting what you want isn't necessarily a solution to your problems. If you're unhappy, and you think, &quot;if only I had ___&quot; - you might be totally wrong, and getting the thing you long for might leave you feeling empty. How am I getting that lesson from a study of young women? Well, to a substantial degree, our culture caters to women, and young women most of all. We give them support and validation and celebrate everything they do. They want to be liked? They want to be popular? We give them social media that quantifies that for them. They want attention? We give them so much attention that, in comparison to what previous generations of women had, is like drinking from a fire hose.</p>\n<p>They have everything they want, but more and more of them are unhappy - not just unhappy but outright depressed.</p>\n<p>See, here's the thing: happiness is a spiritual problem. It's not solved by getting something. It's solved by changing yourself. And this is the relevance to MGTOW. Whatever you decide your &quot;own way&quot; is going to be, it needs to be a path that leads you to happiness. Otherwise, what was the point?</p>","Increasing numbers of young women suffering depression - don't let this happen to you","","mgtow"]]